1. the two routes: a subclass, or a patch
   str.shout = ...  (built-in types are closed)   TypeError
   ShoutStr('hi').shout()                         HI!
   type(ShoutStr('hi') + '!').__name__            str
   type(mock.patch.object(Greeter, 'greet', loud)).__name__ _patch
2. before the patch: Greeter is untouched
   Greeter().greet()                              hello
   Greeter.greet is loud                          False
   hasattr(Greeter, 'shout')                      False
3. inside the with block: replaced
   Greeter().greet()                              HELLO
   [Greeter(), Greeter()] greeted                 ['HELLO', 'HELLO']
   inside_patch()  (a function written in the block) HELLO
   Greeter().greet()  after the block             hello
4. the scope is dynamic: whoever calls while it is active
   before_patch()  (written above the block)      HELLO
   other.call(Greeter())  (another module)        HELLO
   eval('Greeter().greet()')                      HELLO
   inside_patch()  (called from inside again)     HELLO
   inside_patch()  (called after the block)       hello
   decorated()  (the decorator form)              HELLO
   before_patch()  after decorated() returned     hello
5. what reflection sees inside the patch
   hasattr(Greeter(), 'greet')                    True
   Greeter.greet is loud                          True
   vars(Greeter)['greet'] is loud  (the class changed) True
   getattr(Greeter(), 'greet')()                  HELLO
   Greeter().greet.__func__.__name__              loud
   Greeter().greet.__func__.__name__  after       greet
6. the original is kept; a wrapper calls it; built-ins cannot be patched
   patched greet calling the saved original       politely HELLO
   Greeter.greet is original  after               True
   mock.patch.object(str, 'upper', ...)           TypeError
